<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wadi Allaqi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wadi Allaqi is a long dry valley in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-desert">Eastern Desert</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> Hills towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> south of Aswan. It held the richest gold mines of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, worked by Egypt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> and later by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kanz">Banu Kanz</a> chiefs under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Middle Kingdom, Egypt campaigned in Lower Nubia, possibly to check Kerma, and Senusret I built forts at Ikkur, Quban, Aniba, Buhen and Kor to guard the border and the gold mines of <b>Wadi Allaqi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kanz al-Dawla chiefs ruled the southern marches around Aswan as hereditary vassals of the Fatimid Caliphate, controlling trade with Nubia and the Red Sea and the gold of the <b>Wadi Allaqi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-kanz-emirate">Banu Kanz emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>The viceroys collected tribute in gold, ivory, ebony, cattle and soldiers, and Nubian gold mines in the <b>Wadi Allaqi</b> made Egypt the richest power of its age.</blockquote><a href="/state/viceroyalty-of-kush">Viceroyalty of Kush</a></li></ul></section>
